  "schema_version": 1,
  "list_id": "gpt-6-sol",
  "run": 4,
  "simulation_id": "sim-04",
  "member_id": "lindroth-eva",
  "model": "gpt-5.6-terra",
  "reasoning_effort": "high",
  "prompt": "prompts/physics_committee_terra_round2_v1.md",
  "preferred_configuration": {
    "prize_parts": [
      {
        "ballot_id": "B051",
        "laureates": [
          "Paul B. Corkum",
          "Maciej Lewenstein",
          "Kenneth J. Schafer"
        ],
        "citation": "for the electron-recollision model of strong-field atomic physics"
      }
    ]
  },
  "alternatives": [
    "B058",
    "B039"
  ],
  "statement": {
    "case_for": "My configuration is unchanged. B051 is a distinct, fundamental theoretical achievement: the recollision picture made the unexpected high-harmonic response of atoms intelligible as a sequence of tunnel ionization, field acceleration, and recombination. It is not only a useful description of an apparatus, but a durable mechanism whose consequences have repeatedly been tested across strong-field and attosecond physics. Corkum, Lewenstein, and Schafer remain the most coherent proposed allocation for the complementary foundational formulations. The necessary boundary is also scientifically intelligible: this citation concerns the explanatory model of the atomic dynamics, whereas the 2023 award recognized experimental methods for producing and measuring attosecond pulses.",
    "case_against": "The closeness of B051 to the 2023 attosecond award remains a serious reservation. A final historical review must confirm both that the recollision model preceded and is conceptually distinct from the honored experimental methods, and that the three named theorists rather than additional contributors constitute the indispensable attribution. If that boundary or allocation cannot be documented sharply, B058 would be the stronger immediate alternative because its maturity and two-person attribution are unusually clear.",
    "responses": [
      {
        "member_id": "olsson-eva",
        "point": "Olsson considers the recollision framework important and experimentally supported, but judges its boundary from the 2023 attosecond prize insufficiently clean compared with aberration correction.",
        "response": "I agree that the boundary must be exact, and this is why I have narrowed the citation to the electron-recollision model rather than attosecond science generally. A mechanism explaining the nonlinear atomic response is not the same achievement as inventing methods to generate and measure attosecond pulses. The distinction requires documentary confirmation, but it is a physically meaningful one rather than a verbal separation."
      },
      {
        "member_id": "kroll-stefan",
        "point": "Kroll regards B051 as a profound and well-tested framework but gives priority to aberration correction because its prize boundary appears cleaner.",
        "response": "B001 is a formidable experimental alternative, but its allocation among correction theory, independent implementations, instrument teams, and commercialization remains comparably consequential. For B051 the central intellectual object is unusually compact: an electron returns to its parent ion and recombines, producing high harmonics. I would retain it provided the historical roles of the proposed three theorists support that compact citation."
      },
      {
        "member_id": "danielsson-ulf",
        "point": "Danielsson argues that the relation of the recollision theory to the already honored attosecond experimental methods requires more careful examination than the singular discovery of pulsars.",
        "response": "That caution is justified. Pulsars offer an exceptionally clean discovery and Bell Burnell's role deserves close consideration. I nonetheless give priority to B051 because the recollision model revealed a new quantum-dynamical mechanism and supplied a predictive bridge between atomic theory and a broad experimental field; its eligibility turns on defining the earlier theoretical achievement precisely."
      }
    ],
    "uncertainties": [
      "Whether archival review establishes a sufficiently sharp scientific and historical distinction between the recollision model and the experimental methods recognized by the 2023 attosecond prize.",
      "Whether Corkum, Lewenstein, and Schafer alone capture the indispensable foundational contributions to the recollision framework.",
      "Whether B058 can be framed as a discrete foundational physical achievement rather than primarily sustained metrological refinement."
    ],
    "conflict_note": "No candidate-specific conflict is apparent. I have long collaborated with Anne L'Huillier, a 2023 laureate, which makes especially careful treatment of B051's boundary from that prior award appropriate; she is not a proposed laureate here."
  }
}
